WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.374
Endpoints generate valuable telemetry about activity occurring on workstations, servers, and other managed devices.

2
00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:22.519
Depending on the platform and tools in use, events may describe process execution, command activity, file changes, registry modifications, user sessions, services, scheduled tasks, and network connections.

3
00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:32.766
Process telemetry can help analysts understand which program started, which user or parent process initiated it, what arguments were supplied, and what happened next.

4
00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:41.856
File and configuration events can reveal changes to important locations, persistence mechanisms, security settings, or application state.

5
00:00:41.856 --> 00:00:49.329
Endpoint telemetry frequently plays a central role in investigations because it connects user activity with system behavior.

6
00:00:49.329 --> 00:01:01.831
Analysts may use it to establish a timeline, identify related hosts, verify whether a control blocked an action, or determine whether suspicious behavior continued after an initial event.

7
00:01:01.831 --> 00:01:03.567
Coverage still requires care.

8
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:09.764
Devices may be offline, unmanaged, misconfigured, or unable to retain detailed events.

9
00:01:09.764 --> 00:01:19.961
Teams should monitor sensor health, collection policy, time synchronization, parsing, and gaps so that endpoint visibility remains dependable when an incident occurs.

10
00:01:19.961 --> 00:01:25.119
Collection settings should be tested against performance, privacy, and operational needs.

11
00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:32.463
Detailed endpoint data can be valuable, but excessive collection may increase storage, processing, or review costs.

12
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:44.007
Teams should protect sensitive fields, restrict access, and document why higher-detail telemetry is necessary for particular assets, threats, or response requirements.
